      "title": "Study of 100,000 Australians seeks causes of mental health disorders and early onset cancer",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/30/study-of-100000-australians-seeks-causes-of-mental-health-disorders-and-early-onset-cancer",
      "description": "<p>Sax Institute is recruiting for 18 and Up, an intergenerational study of wellbeing and ageing which follows its landmark 45 and Up study</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Researchers hope to better understand the drivers of major health challenges facing younger people, such as mental illness and rising cancer rates, through the recruitment of 100,000 people for Australia’s largest intergenerational study of wellbeing and ageing.</p><p>The independent and not-for-profit research institute the Sax Institute, on Thursday launched the 18 and Up study, which will follow the health of those aged 18 to 45 throughout their lives.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/30/study-of-100000-australians-seeks-causes-of-mental-health-disorders-and-early-onset-cancer\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-29T14:00:48"
    },
    {
      "title": "Erin Patterson prepares for mushroom murders appeal ruling to learn if she could face retrial or longer jail term",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/29/erin-patterson-mushroom-murders-appeal-ruling-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Victorian court to hand down decision on Friday on two appeals regarding Patterson’s triple murder case</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Erin Patterson is set to find out if she could face a longer prison sentence or a new trial, as Victoria’s highest court hands down its decision on two appeals regarding her <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/sep/08/erin-patterson-sentence-australia-mushroom-murder-ntwnfb\">triple murder case</a>.</p><p>The court of appeal is set to rule on the two appeals from 10am on Friday, after justices Lesley Taylor, Stephen McLeish and Peter Kidd heard arguments from her legal team and prosecutors in August.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/29/erin-patterson-mushroom-murders-appeal-ruling-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-29T14:00:47"
    },
    {
      "title": "Lawyers for Marist Brothers face questions over advice to keep serial offender out of ‘legal arena’",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/oct/30/marist-brothers-law-firm-carroll-and-odea-abuse-case-act-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>A once-confidential letter says child sexual abuser might implicate senior church officials if allowed near ‘the legal arena’ upon his release from jail</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Lawyers from a leading law firm for the Marist Brothers have been subpoenaed to explain a once-confidential document showing they advised the Catholic order to keep a serial offender out of “the legal arena”, suggesting he was too honest and might implicate senior church officials.</p><p>The offender was “regrettably up front” about his interactions with senior leadership, and could not be trusted to heed “suggestions” about “not dumping on others excessively”, the document says.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/oct/30/marist-brothers-law-firm-carroll-and-odea-abuse-case-act-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-29T14:00:47"
    },
